A diabetic foot ulcer is a localised injury to the skin and/or underlying tissue below the ankle. Disruption of protective skin allows for colonization of subcutaneous tissues by microbes and in many cases the wound becomes clinically infected, requiring antimicrobial treatment and often some form of surgical intervention.

Diabetic foot ulcers frequently result from a person with diabetes simultaneously having two or more risk factors, with diabetic peripheral neuropathy and peripheral artery disease usually playing a central role.

